2024 was the season registered as the confirmation, and BuildOak edged Alcaraz in it,
inside the interval. The four- and thirteen-week block
checks for 2017–2025 give {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2017_2025/interval_95_block4_log_loss|ci4}} and {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2017_2025/interval_95_block13_log_loss|ci4}}. The second
model alone is {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/second_model_vs_fair_buildoak/y2017_2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/second_model_vs_fair_buildoak/y2017_2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} against BuildOak over 2017–2025;
the previous version alone is level with it.

### The timing rule and what BuildOak's own setup was worth

Historical files give tournament weeks, not exact match days, and results reach the public
with a lag. So a forecast may use only results from at least two days before its match. BuildOak's
own setup used results at once and dated each lower-tier result on the Sunday of its week,
so a Challenger result could reach the next tour event from its first match. Measured on
2017–2024, with BuildOak on the same lower-tier files throughout:

| Change to BuildOak (fair minus its own setup) | Matches | Log loss change |
|---|---:|---:|
| Same timing rule and settings as Alcaraz | {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/fair_minus_native/n|int}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/fair_minus_native/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/fair_minus_native/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} |
| The two-day rule alone | {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/two_day_rule_only_minus_native/n|int}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/two_day_rule_only_minus_native/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/two_day_rule_only_minus_native/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} |
| The rest: rating-recency reference and nationality buckets | {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/fair_minus_two_day_rule_only/n|int}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/fair_minus_two_day_rule_only/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/fair_minus_two_day_rule_only/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} |
| Main-tour files only, same change | {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/main_tour_fair_minus_released/n|int}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/main_tour_fair_minus_released/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/y2017_2024/native_setup_worth/main_tour_fair_minus_released/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} |

A positive change means BuildOak scores worse under the fair rule: its own setup was worth
about that much to it, almost all of it through seeing lower-tier results a few days
sooner. On main-tour files alone the setup is worth nothing. Exact match days would help
both models equally; they are public only in part, so both sides run on the same
conservative rule.

### 2025, with its bug

The 2025 test was written down before any 2025 forecast existed and scored once. It ran
on a defective input. When the second model chose its 2025 settings from 2022–2024, the
file it read had 2024's results blanked, a leftover from when 2024 was sealed. Every
selection row for 2024 carried an invalid label, so it chose badly and its 2025 forecasts
were squashed toward 50/50. No 2025 result reached any model. The independent
recomputation caught the defect; the fix changed nothing but restoring the 2024 labels.

| 2025, {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2025/n|int}} matches | Registered pass (defective input) | Bug fixed |
|---|---:|---:|
| Alcaraz − BuildOak | {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} |
| Holm-adjusted p | {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2025_p_holm|f3}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2025_p_holm|f3}} |
| Alcaraz − previous version | {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/current_vs_previous/y2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/current_vs_previous/y2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_previous/y2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_previous/y2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} |
| Second model − previous version | {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/second_model_vs_previous/y2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/second_model_vs_previous/y2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/second_model_vs_previous/y2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/second_model_vs_previous/y2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} |
| Alcaraz − BuildOak, 2017–2025 | {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2017_2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/registered/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2017_2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} | {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2017_2025/difference/log_loss|s4}} {{cur:fair_buildoak/corrected/current_vs_fair_buildoak/y2017_2025/interval_95_block8/log_loss|ci4}} |

The registered pass read level against BuildOak. With the bug fixed, Alcaraz wins 2025.
The corrected run came after the registered result had been seen, so it is the model's
correct 2025 number but not a blind confirmation. The 2017–2025 result does not depend on
the fix. In the defective run the second model alone was worse than the previous version,
yet the average still beat the previous version, because half its weight sat on a model
the bug did not touch. The rule that came out of it: every forecast generator now asserts
a valid label on every selection row before it selects anything.

### 2014–2016, never scored before

No forecast of these models or of BuildOak had been scored on these seasons. Challenger
serve statistics begin in 2010 and the models need six warm-up seasons, so 2014 is the
earliest season the second model reaches, and 2015 the earliest for the average.

